## Trust

- Everything in this layer is public. The code ships to the browser, so treat the
  source, the env vars, and the network calls as readable by anyone.
- Client-side validation is a courtesy to the user. The server validates again,
  always, because the client can be bypassed with one curl command.
- Never render user-supplied HTML without sanitizing. Never build a URL for
  `href` or `src` from user input without checking the protocol, because
  `javascript:` is a valid protocol.

## State and effects

- Derive rather than duplicate. If a value can be computed from existing state,
  compute it rather than storing a second copy that can drift.
- Every effect that fetches needs a cancellation path and a handled error state.
  A component that only renders the happy path is not finished.
- Loading, empty, error, and partial states are part of the feature. Sketch them
  even in a spike, because retrofitting them means touching every component again.

## Weight

- Check what a new dependency costs before adding it. Name the size in the diff.
- Lazy-load anything below the fold or behind a route.
- Images get explicit dimensions so the layout does not jump.

## Access

- Every interactive element is reachable by keyboard and has an accessible name.
- Do not use color alone to carry meaning.
- Respect `prefers-reduced-motion` on anything that animates.
